# Mobile-Viewable / Responsive UI

Every UI we build must work on phones. Assume users will open the app on mobile first; desktop is an enhancement.

## Approach

- **Mobile-first:** Design and implement the small-screen layout first, then add `sm:`, `md:`, `lg:` (etc.) for larger viewports.
- **Touch-friendly:** Use adequate tap targets (min ~44px), spacing between interactive elements, and avoid hover-only interactions for critical actions.
- **Readable on small screens:** Avoid tiny text; use responsive typography and avoid long fixed-width content.

## Tailwind breakpoints (reference)

- Default: < 640px (mobile)
- `sm:` 640px+
- `md:` 768px+
- `lg:` 1024px+
- `xl:` 1280px+

## Checklist when adding or changing UI

- [ ] Layout stacks or adapts on narrow viewports (no horizontal scroll unless intentional, e.g. carousel).
- [ ] Navigation and key actions are usable on a small screen (e.g. hamburger or bottom nav if many items).
- [ ] Images/media scale or constrain so they don't overflow on mobile.
- [ ] Forms and buttons are easy to tap and don't rely on hover for essential behavior.

When proposing new pages or components, always describe or implement the mobile layout as well as desktop.
